# Cloudflare Pages and Netlify both read a `_headers` file at the site root with this exact syntax: a path pattern
# line followed by indented `Header: value` lines, `*` as a greedy wildcard (usable mid-segment, e.g. `*.wasm`,
# per Netlify's documented `/templates/*.html` example — Cloudflare Pages' docs describe the same splat mechanism
# and state that headers from every matching rule apply, later values for the same header appending rather than
# replacing). See https://docs.netlify.com/manage/routing/headers/ and
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/.
#
# The `/*` block mirrors RESPONSE_HEADERS in main.ts's dev server exactly. Cross-origin isolation (COOP/COEP/CORP) is
# required for SharedArrayBuffer, which the threaded Wasm core needs (src/core/wasm/loader.ts::planCore). Every
# resource the app loads is same-origin, so these headers cost nothing; without them the app falls back to the
# single-thread core. The one cross-origin script, the Cloudflare Web Analytics beacon that Pages injects, is served
# with `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin`, so isolation admits it.
#
# `Content-Security-Policy` lets the page load code only from its own origin and that beacon, and send data only to its
# own origin, Cloudflare Web Analytics and PostHog (the usage counter, src/ui/analytics.ts), so the browser enforces that a recording and its print stay on the device.
# main.ts explains each source; tests/unit/server.test.ts checks this block against main.ts and the inline-script
# hashes against static/*.html, and tests/browser/csp.test.ts checks that it blocks nothing the app needs.
#
# `Cache-Control: no-cache` lets a browser keep a copy but makes it revalidate before every use (a 304 when nothing
# changed). dist/assets/* keep the same filenames across deploys (scripts/build.ts does not content-hash them), so
# any freshness lifetime would let a browser pair a new index.html with the previous deploy's JS or Wasm. It is set in
# this one rule only: a second matching rule would append its value instead of replacing this one, and a `/*.html`
# rule would not reach the pages anyway, because Cloudflare Pages serves index.html at `/` and answers `/index.html`
# with a 308 redirect.
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Cache-Control: no-cache
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' 'sha256-72b/DD+w57zZTw1/sy4gku92PAJe1ygBgaDKzY/Zddk=' 'sha256-PGCb9wneg1HTtNXK0aq4Xv6WfzgUe5QpGX9e1U/Je6A=' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; media-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com https://us.i.posthog.com; form-action 'none'

# Belt-and-braces content type for the Wasm core builds: some hosts' built-in MIME inference falls back to
# application/octet-stream for .wasm, which WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming() refuses to compile from directly.
/assets/*.wasm
  Content-Type: application/wasm
